<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madiun Affair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Madiun uprising · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-revolution">Indonesian National Revolution</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madiun">Madiun</a> Affair was a communist uprising in September 1948 centred on the town of Madiun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>'s war of independence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Leftist troops and supporters of the Communist Party, whose leader Musso had recently returned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, seized the town and proclaimed a new government. The Republican army crushed the revolt within weeks and Musso was killed, leaving lasting hostility between the army and the communists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madiun-affair.jpg" alt="Madiun Affair" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nationaal Archief · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Groep_IIndonesische_mannen_wordt_met_touwen_geboeid_en_onder_bewaking_afgevoerd_,_Bestanddeelnr_8806.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Amir Sjarifuddin's cabinet fell on 28 January 1948, and his left-wing faction refused posts in Hatta's new cabinet unless it kept key ministries, including defence. In February the left regrouped at Surakarta as the People's Democratic Front (FDR), led by Sjarifuddin and joining the Partai Sosialis, the PKI, Pesindo and the labour federation SOBSI. It opposed the Renville Agreement, which Hatta meant to carry out, and Hatta's rationalisation of the army, which cut excess troops and demobilised the TNI Masjarakat, a local force that had been a base of Sjarifuddin's strength.</p><p>Tension grew through mid-1948: the Senopati Division of Solo resisted rationalisation, its commander Colonel Sutarto was murdered on 2 July, and some 20,000 workers struck at the Delanggu cotton plantations for about 35 days. On 11 August Suripno returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> with Musso, who became chairman of a new political bureau of the left late in August. In September kidnappings and clashes between pro-government units such as the Siliwangi Division and leftist groups left Solo in government hands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madiun">Madiun</a> the FDR's last important stronghold.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At 3 am on 18 September 1948 FDR forces under Sumarsono and Djoko Sujono seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madiun">Madiun</a>'s government offices, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> exchange and army headquarters; two loyal officers were killed. Setyadjit and Wikana set up a regional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-front-czechoslovakia">National Front</a> government, and Sumarsono announced on the radio, From Madiun victory begins. On the night of 19 September President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> called the revolt an attempt to overthrow the Republic and told Indonesians to choose between him and Hatta or Musso, backed by Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-ix">Hamengkubuwono IX</a>; Musso answered by declaring war on the government. Some FDR leaders, including Sumarsono and Sjarifuddin, then insisted that it was no coup.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The government used the revolt to crack down on the left. Troops under Colonel Gatot Soebroto and Nasution moved in, and Lieutenant Colonel Sadikin of the Siliwangi Division was sent to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madiun">Madiun</a> on 30 September. The FDR leaders retreated towards the hills. On 28 October the government arrested 1,500 men of the last rebel unit, and Musso was shot dead three days later; his body was displayed at Ponorogo and burned. Sjarifuddin was captured on 4 December and executed with other FDR leaders on 19 December. About 35,000 people were arrested, and casualties were estimated at 24,000 across Madiun, Cepu and Ponorogo. Sumarsono escaped, and was arrested again in the anti-communist campaign of 1965.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Madiun Affair” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Netherlands refused to recognise the republic, and four years of revolution followed, marked by the Battle of Surabaya against British forces in November 1945, two Dutch military offensives in 1947 and 1948, and the communist <b>Madiun Affair</b> of 1948.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
